{
    "background_story": "Damian was a 17th-century Spanish poet known for his elaborate and intricate writing style, earning him the label of a Gongorist. His poetry was a maze of complex metaphors, clever wordplay, and rich imagery, which both fascinated and intimidated his contemporaries. Damian's work was heavily influenced by the Baroque movement, and he often explored themes of love, death, and the human condition. His most famous poem, 'The Echoes of Eternity', was a masterpiece of convoluted syntax and poetic license, which sparked both admiration and criticism from the literary circles of his time. Despite the controversy surrounding his style, Damian remained true to his artistic vision, leaving behind a legacy of innovative and challenging poetry that continues to inspire and puzzle readers to this day.",
